Playwright Framework Guidelines & Code Quality Standards
1. Project Structure
Follow a clear, modular folder structure:
├── tests/                   # Feature/BDD specs or test files
├── pages/                   # Page Object Models (POMs)
├── locators/                # Locators/constants separated per page/module
├── utils/                   # Utilities/helpers (API calls, date utils, etc.)
├── fixtures/                # Test data / mock data
├── config/                  # Playwright config, environment-specific settings
├── reports/                 # Test execution reports (HTML/Allure, etc.)
Keep test logic separate from locators and utilities.
2. Naming Conventions
Files/Folders → camelCase for files, PascalCase for class names.
loginPage.ts, customerDetailsPage.ts, srValidator.ts
Classes → PascalCase (LoginPage, SRValidator)
Functions/Methods → camelCase (clickOnLoginButton())
Locators object → UPPER_SNAKE_CASE for object name, camelCase for keys:
export const CREATE_SK_LOCATORS = { searchInput: "st-search-ip" };
Text constants → UPPER_SNAKE_CASE for both object and keys:
export const TEXT_CONSTANTS = { SEARCH_BUTTON: "Search" };
3. Page Object Model (POM)
Each page gets its own class.
Methods represent actions, not element interactions:
❌ await page.click('#login-button')
✅ await loginPage.login(username, password)
Locators never hard-coded inside test — always in locators.ts or class properties.
4. Assertions
Use Playwright expect API (await expect(locator).toBeVisible()) instead of manual checks like .isVisible() === true.
Avoid multiple expectations in a single test unless logically grouped.
Write clear assertion messages (where applicable):
await expect(locator, "Login button should be visible").toBeVisible();
5. Selectors & Locators
Prefer data-testid attributes → stable & independent of UI changes.
Fallback: getByRole, getByText, .filter().
Avoid brittle selectors (nth-child, deep DOM chains).
Example:
page.getByTestId('search-button');
page.getByRole('button', { name: /Search/i });
6. Constants & Test Data
Keep UI text in TEXT_CONSTANTS object.
Keep API endpoints in a dedicated api.endpoints.ts.
Environment/configuration should come from config.ts or process.env.
7. Error Handling
Wrap reusable actions with safe error handling:
async safeClick(locator: Locator) {
  await locator.waitFor({ state: "visible" });
  await locator.click();
}
8. Code Quality Rules
✅ One test = one logical scenario.
✅ Avoid copy-paste → extract common flows into helper functions.
✅ Reuse page methods, don’t duplicate locators.
✅ Always add return types (Promise<void>, Promise<string>).
✅ Prefer async/await consistently.
❌ Don’t use hard waits (waitForTimeout), prefer waitForLoadState, expect().toBeVisible().
❌ Don’t mix test data into test steps → keep in fixtures or constants.
9. Reusable Utilities
Create utils for:
API calls (apiService.ts)
Date/time formatting
Random data generation (e.g., faker.js)
Never write raw logic in test files.
10. Reviews & Standards
Every PR should have:
✅ Readable test case description
✅ No hardcoded values (unless test-specific)
✅ Proper use of POM & constants
✅ Linting/prettier applied
11. Reporting & CI/CD
Use HTML reporter / Allure for detailed reports.
Integrate with CI (Jenkins/GitHub Actions).
Ensure tests can run in parallel and are idempotent (independent).
12. BDD (if using Cucumber)
Step definitions should call Page methods only, not raw selectors.
Feature files should be business-readable, not technical.
✅ Following this will give you a scalable, maintainable Playwright framework aligned with best practices.
